{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.75,
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 13, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"}
      ],
      "why": "pipelined is the only family that has produced feasible designs (23/44) and owns 9/9 of the current merged front, so it is where the trade-off curve actually lives. Its per-stage critical path is one fixed-shift micro-rotation (wired shifts, no barrel shifter), so Fmax is high and the burst requirement (32 requests from one 1 MHz tick inside 0.5 us = ~(N+2+31)/Fmax) is met with large margin; the binding constraint is accuracy vs. area, not timing. The selection rule is min area_um2, so the useful region is the smallest (W,N) that still satisfies max_abs_err <= 2^-12: output LSB is 2^-(W-2), so W>=14 is essentially forced, and the residual-angle term needs N>=~13. Ranges 13..20 / 10..20 bracket that boundary (some cheap low-N, low-W probes to find the true feasible edge) while still reaching the high-accuracy end (W=20 gives ~16 accuracy_bits, which is what pulls the HV reference point at accuracy 12). Guards and rounding are kept fully open because they are the second-order accuracy levers and cost almost nothing in area."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.25,
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 13, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 2, "param": "m"}
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m computes identical bits to pipelined but registers only ceil(N/m) stages instead of N, so it is the one family that can undercut pipelined on the min-area objective while still delivering 1 result/cycle. Its 0/56 record is almost certainly a timing artefact: it spends m micro-rotations per registered stage, so Fmax falls roughly with m, and at larger m the analytic burst bound (N+2+31 cycles inside 0.5 us) becomes the binding constraint while accuracy is unchanged. That is testable cheaply by restricting m to 2..4, where the combinational path is at most 4 micro-rotations and Fmax should stay in the same band as pipelined, and by reusing exactly the accuracy window that pipelined proved feasible (13..20 / 10..20). If any pipelined_m point lands on the front it wins on area; if the small-m probe again yields nothing feasible, that fact is decisive and the remaining budget should go entirely to pipelined."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is min-area subject to max_abs_err <= 2^-12 and a burst constraint (32 simultaneous requests per 1 us tick, all back within 0.5 us). Only pipelined has ever produced feasible points and it holds the entire merged front, so the fleet budget should be concentrated there; unrolled_k/iterative-style sharing is not in my remit anyway and is already 0/100 across the fleet. The burst constraint is not the bottleneck for pipelined: the schedule is N+2 latency then 1 result/cycle, so a 32-request burst drains in about N+33 cycles, i.e. roughly 100 MHz suffices for N~20, well under any plausible Fmax for a single-micro-rotation stage. That makes accuracy-vs-area the only real trade-off, with area growing roughly as N x (W+frac_guard) plus N x 3(W+frac_guard) flops. I therefore sweep the accuracy-parameter space that defines the front (W, N, guards, rounding) rather than the timing space, centred on the smallest feasible (W,N) corner that the selection rule will pick, and still reaching far enough into the high-accuracy corner (W=20, N=20 -> ~16 accuracy_bits) to give the hypervolume calculation something to cover against the accuracy-12 reference. pipelined_m gets a deliberately small probe at m=2..4, since fewer registers is the only structural way to beat pipelined on the min-area objective; if that probe is feasible it should appear at the low-area end of the front, and if it is not, the evidence is strong enough to spend everything else on pipelined."
}

{
  "decision": "map_front",
  "next_plan": null
,
  "rationale": "The feasible front is entirely `pipelined` and covers only data_width 19..20 of the registry's 8..28 — a very small slice of the accuracy/area dimensionality. Accuracy on the front runs 12.8..16.8 bits while the constraint floor is 12, so the whole low-accuracy/low-area corner (the region that actually pays off under `select: min area_um2` and that extends the front toward the HV reference at 12) is unexplored; the min-area point 43452 sits at the n_iter=14 edge of the explored range, so cheaper designs almost certainly exist at data_width~17-18 or n_iter~12-13. The other objectives are clearly non-binding (throughput 0% violate, best 156 >= 32; sys_p99_batch 24% violate, best 0.289 <= 0.5), so the trade-off to map is purely area vs accuracy. pipelined_m contributed 0 feasible in 25 evals and iterative/unrolled_k are not on the front (their Fmax/(N+3) throughput cannot reach 32 MSPS at the N>=13 needed for 12 bits), so mapping should focus the full parameter ranges seeded from the current pipelined front. This is the reserved full-range mapping pass that covers the objective curve rather than just the corner the selection rule picks."
}
